 \begin{abstract}
-Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) in law must trace multi-hop precedent chains and must not present overruled doctrine as current law, yet common evaluators reward entity overlap rather than grounded reasoning. We introduce TERRA (historically named Temporal-Evolution and Reasoning-Trace RAG), an experimental dual-phase GraphRAG architecture. Offline, it builds an Event Evolution Graph over \CorpusTotalOpinions\ Supreme Court opinions connected by \CorpusPrecedentEdges\ directed citation edges, plus an index of generic procedural scaffolds. Online, a small-model Traffic Cop router sends single-point lookups down a lighter fast path (retrieval and an entailment check, without graph traversal) and multi-hop questions through citation traversal, and an LLM-prompted entailment grader evaluates retrieved evidence before generation. We evaluate on a \numUniqueQueries-query benchmark against four baselines (\numTotalEvals\ evaluations) using two non-Llama judges, paired Wilcoxon tests and Benjamini--Hochberg correction. TERRA scores higher overall faithfulness ($\TerraOverallFaithfulnessMean$ vs.\ $\HippoRAGOverallFaithfulnessMean$ for the next best system, overlapping 95\% CIs) and answer-only faithfulness ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulnessMean$, computed on differing answered subsets), and significantly outperforms all three retrieval baselines on Category~B multi-hop reasoning ($\Delta$ from $\CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$ [HippoRAG] and $\CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$ [Flat] to $\CatBTerraVsGraphRAGDelta$ [GraphRAG]; FDR $q<0.05$; Flat CI includes zero), although a no-retrieval Direct LLM scores significantly higher on Category~B ($\Delta=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMDelta$, $q=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMAdjQ$; TERRA's Category~B faithfulness is low in absolute terms, $\TerraCatBFaithfulness$). On adversarial Category~D queries TERRA safely handles \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate\ of traps (\TerraCatDRejectionRate\ explicit refusals, \TerraCatDRefutationRate\ verified refutations) with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors, but refuses \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ Category~A and \TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ Category~B queries, at \TerraLatencyMedianSeconds~s median latency. On \ObscCohortSize\ obscure overruled precedents, Direct LLM safe handling falls to \ObscDirectSafePct\ versus \ObscFullSafePct\ for full TERRA, but TERRA without curated overruling labels drops to \ObscUnlabeledSafePct, not significantly different from flat retrieval ($p\,\ObscPrimaryMcNemarPRel$); safety depends on curated annotations. Absent licensed attorney evaluation, human review remains an essential deployment prerequisite.
