 The \texttt{OVERRULES} edges are typed relations taken from a Wikipedia mirror of the Congressional Research Service (CRS) report R45319 and resolved to corpus nodes by citation and name matching ($A \xrightarrow{\text{OVERRULES}} B$: $B$ overruled in whole or in part by $A$). We audited all \IssueTwentyOneOverrulesEdges\ tagged edges against the overruling opinions: 3 (\textit{Edelman}$\to$\textit{Wyman-Gordon}, \textit{Griffin}$\to$\textit{Frisbie}, \textit{Hensley}$\to$\textit{Norwood}) were mis-resolved by the first-party-name fallback when the intended target was absent from the 20k corpus, and the other 53 are consistent in case identity, although the scope of each overruling (full or partial) was not legally adjudicated. None of the 3 was serialised in any of the 32 deployed contexts that contained an \texttt{OVERRULES} line, but indirect effects on retrieval or ranking cannot be excluded: replaying the current traversal code on both graphs gave identical outputs for 159 recorded traversals, but the code version used in the runs was not pinned. All reported results use the frozen index; a corrected derivative without the 3 edges (145,363 edges; SHA256 \texttt{14827bd6...}) is retained as a separately versioned local derivative and is not used by any reported run; public release is pending. The 19 Category-D queries (e.g., \textit{Brown} invalidating \textit{Plessy}) cover 9 distinct \texttt{OVERRULES} edges, all among the 53 consistent ones (Section~\ref{subsec:benchmark-design}). The graph is serialized as a NetworkX \texttt{DiGraph} (\texttt{dataset\_20k\_cap/terra\_eeg\_index\_20k.json}).
 
 \subsubsection{Generic Procedural Scaffold Construction and Vector Serialization}
-Each evaluated case is paired with a generic procedural scaffold built from Harvard Caselaw Access Project (CAP) metadata. The historical Structured Legal Reasoning Trace (SLRT) name does not denote case-specific legal reasoning:
+Each evaluated case is paired with a generic procedural scaffold built from Harvard Caselaw Access Project (CAP) metadata, not a case-specific legal reasoning trace:
 \begin{enumerate}
     \item \textbf{Curated Landmark Prototypes ($N=\TraceCuratedCount$):} during early development, \TraceCuratedCount\ landmark decisions received curated traces generated by instruction-tuned models (\texttt{gemini-3.1-flash-lite} baseline; \texttt{mistral.ministral-3-8b-instruct} variants for benchmark seeds) at $T=0.0$, capped at 150 words and following a five-point schema (constitutional question, governing provisions and standard of review, holding, precedent connection, longitudinal significance). They are not in the evaluated index.
     \item \textbf{Deterministic Procedural Substrate ($N=\TraceProceduralCount$, \TraceProceduralPct):} all \TraceProceduralCount\ opinions are serialized by a procedural function (\texttt{build\_structured\_trace}) from the CAP case title (\texttt{name\_abbreviation}), decision year (\texttt{decision\_date}) and a domain phrase. Every node receives the same five-step template (legal question, governing precedent, factual record, harmonizing doctrines, holding), with no case-specific issue, procedural history, test or holding. The domain phrase differs from the node's \texttt{category} field for 18{,}247 of 20{,}000 nodes (19{,}298 read ``Civil Rights Equal Protection'', although 1{,}153 nodes carry that category). The evaluated index supplies generic procedural scaffolds, not LLM-generated case-specific reasoning.
